{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates the variance of the lotteries. Variance measures the spread of possible outcomes around the expected value. For Lottery A: outcome is 5 Euros with 100% probability, so variance is 0 (no risk). For Lottery B: outcome is 5000 Euros with 0.1% probability and 0 Euros with 99.9% probability. The variance is calculated as: (5000 - E[X])² × 0.001 + (0 - E[X])² × 0.999. Expected value of B is 5000 × 0.001 = 5 Euros. So variance = (5000 - 5)² × 0.001 + (0 - 5)² × 0.999 = (4995)² × 0.001 + 25 × 0.999 ≈ 24,950,000 × 0.001 + 24.975 ≈ 24,950 + 24.975 ≈ 24,975. So Lottery B has a much higher variance than Lottery A. The decision reason prefers the lottery with the smaller variance, which is Lottery A. Thus, the reason is applicable to the decision problem and clearly applies to the comparison of A and B.",
